<%!-- PWA app-shell scroll lock: the shell occupies exactly the full
     viewport, matching the `html, body` height rules in app.css for
     both display modes:

     **Browser tab (default)** — `h-[100dvh]`. iOS Safari + Chrome show
     a dynamic bottom URL/toolbar; `100vh` reports the largest viewport
     (toolbar retracted) so a flex-rooted bottom-nav inside a `100vh`
     shell hides BEHIND the toolbar when it's expanded. `100dvh` tracks
     the visible viewport and keeps the bottom-nav above the toolbar.

     **Installed PWA (`display-mode: standalone`)** — the override is
     not a class here. `app-shell-root` puts this element into the
     `@media (display-mode: standalone)` rule in app.css, which sets
     `height: min(100vh, calc(100dvh + env(safe-area-inset-top, 0px)))`
     on `html`, `body` and this root together. One definition for the
     standalone height is the point. It used to live twice, once as a
     Tailwind arbitrary variant on this element and once in app.css,
     with a comment asking the reader to hand-check that they matched;
     now they cannot disagree. The app.css comment carries the full
     reasoning: the May 2026 iPhone 16 on-device measurement that rules
     out plain `100dvh`, and why `min()` cannot lengthen the shell past
     the `100vh` that measurement established. (Task 019ffdce, Android
     on-device report 2026-08-17.)

     `overflow-hidden` prevents the document itself from scrolling;
     combined with the html/body lock in app.css, only the inner
     content scroller (per-LiveView, `overflow-y-auto`) can scroll, so
     the chrome stays put on mobile.

     Insets: `safe-area-top` padding below clears the top Dynamic Island;
     the home-indicator zone is handled by `padding-bottom:
     env(safe-area-inset-bottom)` on `.bottom-nav` itself (app.css) — the
     nav background flows into the gesture zone while the icons stay at
     `flex-end` above it. Fixed-position panels (e.g. tasks/skills detail)
     bound their bottom edge to `var(--bottom-nav-height)` so they don't
     sit behind the nav. --%>
<div
  class="app-shell-root flex h-[100dvh] overflow-hidden bg-base-100 safe-area-top pb-[var(--meeting-bar-height,0px)]"
  style={"--meeting-bar-height: " <> (if @meeting_active, do: "3rem", else: "0px")}
>
  <%!-- Per-org scope colours as CSS custom properties. Rendered here, once,
       because every surface that shows a scope chip renders this shell.
       Deliberately NOT phx-update="ignore": an admin recolouring an org has
       to reach the next render, and an ignored subtree would swallow it.
       See PlatformWeb.Components.ScopeColorStyles for why this is a style
       element and not a Tailwind utility. --%>
  <.scope_color_styles viewer={assigns[:current_user_record]} />
  <%!-- Sidebar collapse persistence hook (localStorage) --%>
  <div
    id="sidebar-collapse-hook"
    phx-hook="SidebarCollapse"
    data-collapsed={if @sidebar_collapsed, do: "true", else: "false"}
    class="hidden"
  >
  </div>
  <%!-- Per-module route resume: persists window.location.pathname on every
       LV nav under `route_resume:<module>` in localStorage, and intercepts
       clicks on any `<a data-module-id=…>` (top-nav tiles) to route to the
       saved URL rather than the module root. Singleton hook — one instance
       covers the whole shell. See `apps/platform/assets/js/hooks/route_resume.js`. --%>
  <div id="route-resume" phx-hook="RouteResume" class="hidden"></div>
  <%!-- Unread-count badge mirror. Singleton hook, same pattern as the two
       above. Markup and rationale live on `Layouts.app_badge_mount/1`'s
       @doc — shared with the `:external` layout. --%>
  <.app_badge_mount />
  <%!-- Web-push registration (task 019ffb7b stage 3). Singleton hook, one
       instance per page, covering every surface that renders this shell —
       Tasks, Inbox, Meetings, Activity, the lot. It used to live in
       `chat_live.html.heex` behind an `:if={!@embedded}` guard, which bound
       registration to `/chat` alone: a user working from any other route
       mounted no hook, reported no permission state, and could never be
       offered the opt-in. The guard existed only to stop a duplicate DOM id
       when ChatLive mounts inside MeetingLive, and a shell-level anchor
       cannot collide with itself, so it is deliberately not reproduced here.

       `data-permission` mirrors the server's `:push_permission` assign back
       into the DOM. The hook does not read it (there is no `updated()`
       callback); it exists so the wire state is observable — in tests via
       `has_element?/2`, and in a browser via the element inspector — on
       routes that render nothing else off the assign. The visible consumers
       are the `/inbox` notification-state row
       (`PlatformWeb.Components.NotificationState`) and the chat banner.

       Handlers live in `PlatformWeb.ShellLive`'s `:push_events` attach, not
       in this template's LiveViews, because the `:external_chat` and
       `:meet_member` live_sessions run the same on_mount chain while
       rendering a chrome-less layout. --%>
  <div
    id="push-subscribe"
    phx-hook="PushSubscribe"
    data-permission={@push_permission}
    class="hidden"
  >
  </div>
  <%!-- Desktop icon-rail (lg+ width). Single-source-of-truth destination
       list lives in PlatformWeb.Components.IconRail.destinations/1; the
       template here just hands off current_path / root_user /
       sidebar_collapsed and lets the component own the rendering. Mobile
       gets the matching `<.bottom_rail>` below. Admin tiles are gated by
       `root_user` and never render in the DOM for non-root users (task
       019e389b Stage 3 — replaces the inline 11-link sidebar). --%>
  <.icon_rail
    current_path={@current_path}
    root_user={@root_user}
    sidebar_collapsed={@sidebar_collapsed}
    inbox_unread_count={@inbox_unread_count}
    chat_unread_count={@chat_unread_count}
    brand_name={@brand_name}
  />

  <%!-- Main content area. min-w-0 + min-h-0: this column is flex-1 inside
       a flex row (the shell root) AND a flex-col itself, so its children
       (header, main, bottom-nav) need a bounded parent. Without min-h-0
       the column inherits min-height: auto and grows to its intrinsic
       content height, which on long chat threads pushes the bottom-tab
       nav out of the visible viewport on iOS. --%>
  <div class="flex flex-1 flex-col overflow-hidden min-w-0 min-h-0">
    <%!-- Top bar --%>
    <%!-- `relative` is load-bearing for the roster dropdown below, not
         decoration: the dropdown anchors to the HEADER's right edge rather
         than to its trigger button, so it can never be pushed off the left
         of the viewport by a trigger that sits mid-strip. See the panel's
         own comment for the measurement. --%>
    <header
      id="utility-bar"
      class="relative flex h-10 items-center justify-between border-b border-edge bg-base-200/60 px-4"
    >
      <%!-- min-w-0 + flex-1 here and shrink-0 on the right cluster (task
           01a1261e stage 3): when the native install switcher shows at
           375px, the LEFT side truncates; the roster and user controls
           never squeeze or wrap. --%>
      <div class="flex min-w-0 flex-1 items-center gap-2">
        <%!-- Mobile primary nav is the bottom-nav from #13; the original
             hamburger + drawer pattern was deleted in task 019e0d4b Stage 2. --%>
        <%!-- Active module name — desktop too since task 019f575e stage 3:
             the brand moved into the rail header, so the bar carries the
             current-module context instead of a second brand mark. --%>
        <%!-- Native install switcher (task 01a1261e stage 3). Rendered
             `hidden` for EVERY client; only the NativeInstallSwitch hook
             reveals it, and only inside the Burrow app on a build whose
             SuiteNative plugin has `installBadges` (desktop, PWA and older
             app builds see no change). Tapping opens the app's install
             picker. The dot (no number) means another signed-in install has
             unread; slot `bg-primary`, the chat rail's
             `.channel-row-unread-pill` slot in dot form. The logo resolves
             through `/branding/current/apple_touch`, which redirects to the
             shipped Burrow mark when the install has uploaded none. --%>
        <button
          id="native-install-switch"
          type="button"
          data-role="native-install-switch"
          phx-hook="NativeInstallSwitch"
          aria-label={"Switch install, current: #{@brand_name}"}
          class="hidden -ml-1 shrink-0 items-center gap-1.5 rounded-md px-1 py-0.5 hover:bg-base-300/60"
        >
          <img
            src="/branding/current/apple_touch"
            alt=""
            class="size-5 shrink-0 rounded"
            data-role="install-logo"
          />
          <span class="max-w-[7rem] truncate text-sm font-semibold">{@brand_name}</span>
          <span class="hero-chevron-down size-4 shrink-0 text-base-content/60" aria-hidden="true">
          </span>
          <span
            data-role="install-unread-dot"
            class="hidden size-2 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-primary"
            aria-hidden="true"
          >
          </span>
        </button>
        <span class="min-w-0 truncate text-sm font-semibold">
          {@active_module}
        </span>
      </div>
      <div class="flex shrink-0 items-center gap-2">
        <%!-- Roster presence indicator. Wrapped in <.async_result> so the
             initial assign_async load shows a spinner without blocking page
             paint. After PubSub-driven refreshes (refresh_roster/2) the
             assigns remain in %AsyncResult{ok?: true} shape, so this
             component continues to render the resolved branch. --%>
        <.async_result :let={principal_name} assign={@principal_name}>
          <:loading>
            <span
              class="loading loading-spinner loading-xs text-base-content/40"
              data-test-id="shell-roster-loading"
              title="Loading roster"
            >
            </span>
          </:loading>
          <:failed>
            <%!-- Neutral fallback so a transient DB blip doesn't blank the bar --%>
            <span
              class="inline-block size-2 rounded-full bg-base-content/20"
              data-test-id="shell-roster-failed"
              title="Roster unavailable"
            >
            </span>
          </:failed>
          <%= if principal_name do %>
            <div>
              <button
                phx-click="toggle_roster"
                class="flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-md px-2 py-1 text-xs font-medium text-base-content/70 hover:bg-base-300 transition-colors"
                title="Agent roster"
                data-test-id="shell-roster-button"
              >
                <.async_result :let={composite} assign={@composite_status}>
                  <:loading>
                    <span class="loading loading-spinner loading-xs"></span>
                  </:loading>
                  <:failed>
                    <%!-- Never a number here. A zero is a claim, and "the
                         roster query failed" is not the claim "no agents". --%>
                    <span
                      class="inline-block size-2 rounded-full bg-neutral"
                      data-test-id="shell-composite-failed"
                    />
                    <span class="text-base-content/50">Agents unavailable</span>
                  </:failed>
                  <%!-- `composite` is the `%{healthy:, total:, state:}` map
                       from `SpaceAgentPresence.composite/1`. `dot_color/1`
                       takes the STATE atom — handing it the map falls through
                       to the catch-all and renders neutral forever, which is
                       how the dot became decorative. --%>
                  <span
                    class={[
                      "inline-block size-2 rounded-full",
                      Platform.Chat.SpaceAgentPresence.dot_color(composite.state)
                    ]}
                    data-test-id="shell-agents-dot"
                  />
                  <%= if composite.total > 0 do %>
                    <%!-- Tabular mono figures: the ratio updates live, and
                         proportional digits make it twitch sideways. --%>
                    <span
                      class="font-mono tabular-nums font-bold"
                      data-test-id="shell-agents-ratio"
                    >
                      {composite.healthy}/{composite.total}
                    </span>
                    <span>agents</span>
                  <% else %>
                    <span>Agents</span>
                  <% end %>
                </.async_result>
              </button>

              <%!-- Roster dropdown panel --%>
              <%!-- Anchored to the HEADER (`relative` above), not to the
                   trigger. Anchored to the button this panel measured
                   `left: -60.2px` at a 375px viewport: `right-0` pinned its
                   right edge to a button sitting mid-strip at x≈196, so a
                   256px panel started 60px off-screen and every name was
                   clipped to its tail. Note the `max-w-` clamp does NOT catch
                   that — 256px is already under the cap, so the rule never
                   engages — and neither does an overflow check, because
                   leftward overflow produces no scrollbar and leaves
                   `scrollWidth == clientWidth`. The measurement that fails is
                   `getBoundingClientRect().left >= 0`. Against the header's
                   right edge, left is `viewport - 1rem - 16rem` = 103px at
                   375px, and grows from there. --%>
              <div
                :if={assigns[:roster_open] && @roster_open}
                class="absolute right-4 top-full mt-1 z-50 w-64 max-w-[calc(100vw-2rem)] rounded-lg border border-edge bg-base-200 shadow-lg"
                data-test-id="shell-roster-panel"
              >
                <div class="p-3 space-y-1">
                  <.async_result :let={composite} assign={@composite_status}>
                    <:loading></:loading>
                    <:failed></:failed>
                    <div
                      :if={composite.total > 0}
                      class="flex items-center gap-2 px-2 pb-1 text-[10px] uppercase tracking-widest text-base-content/40"
                      data-test-id="shell-roster-panel-header"
                    >
                      <span>Agents</span>
                      <span class="font-mono tabular-nums">
                        {composite.healthy} of {composite.total} linked
                      </span>
                    </div>
                  </.async_result>
                  <.async_result :let={roster_items} assign={@roster_items}>
                    <:loading>
                      <div class="flex items-center justify-center py-4">
                        <span class="loading loading-spinner loading-sm"></span>
                      </div>
                    </:loading>
                    <:failed>
                      <div class="text-xs text-base-content/40 px-2 py-2">
                        Roster unavailable
                      </div>
                    </:failed>
                    <%= for {sa, status} <- roster_items do %>
                      <div class="flex items-center justify-between py-1.5 px-2 rounded hover:bg-base-300 group">
                        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
                          <%!-- No dot for an orphan. A status dot reading
                               "offline" is a false claim about a record that
                               no longer exists — there is nothing there to be
                               offline. --%>
                          <span
                            :if={sa.agent}
                            class={[
                              "inline-block size-2 rounded-full",
                              Platform.Chat.SpaceAgentPresence.dot_color(status)
                            ]}
                          />
                          <%!-- `roster_entry.agent` is contract-nullable: a
                          participant row can outlive its agent row. Render
                          the orphan rather than dropping it, so it stays
                          visible and removable. "Deleted agent", not
                          "Unknown agent": the latter describes a naming
                          problem, and the fact is a deleted record. --%>
                          <span class="text-sm font-medium">
                            {cond do
                              sa.agent == nil -> "Deleted agent"
                              sa.role == "principal" -> sa.agent.name
                              true -> "@#{sa.agent.name}"
                            end}
                          </span>
                          <span
                            :if={sa.role == "principal"}
                            class="text-[10px] text-base-content/40 uppercase"
                          >
                            principal
                          </span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
                          <span :if={sa.agent} class="text-xs text-base-content/50">
                            {Platform.Chat.SpaceAgentPresence.status_label(status)}
                          </span>
                          <button
                            :if={sa.role != "principal"}
                            phx-click="dismiss_agent"
                            phx-value-agent-id={sa.agent_id}
                            phx-value-space-id={sa.space_id}
                            class="hidden group-hover:inline text-xs text-base-content/40 hover:text-error transition-colors"
                            title="Remove agent"
                          >
                            Remove
                          </button>
                        </div>
                      </div>
                    <% end %>
                  </.async_result>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          <% else %>
            <%!-- Legacy status dot (no space / no principal configured) --%>
            <span
              :if={assigns[:agent_status]}
              class={"inline-block size-2 rounded-full #{agent_status_color(@agent_status)}"}
            >
            </span>
            <span :if={assigns[:agent_status]} class="text-xs text-base-content/50">
              {agent_status_label(@agent_status)}
            </span>
          <% end %>
        </.async_result>

        <%!-- Human presence indicator --%>
        <%!-- The resolved value is a three-state `{:ok, list} | {:error, reason}`.
              Every entry in the viewer union is online by construction, so the
              badge count is literally `length(list)` and cannot disagree with the
              rows. A failed read is NEVER rendered as a number: an unavailable
              backend and an empty office must not look the same. --%>
        <.async_result :let={presence_result} assign={@presence_list}>
          <:loading>
            <span
              class="loading loading-spinner loading-xs text-base-content/40"
              data-test-id="shell-presence-loading"
              title="Loading participants"
            >
            </span>
          </:loading>
          <div class="relative">
            <button
              phx-click="toggle_presence_panel"
              data-test-id="shell-presence-badge"
              class="flex items-center gap-1 text-xs text-base-content/50 hover:text-base-content transition-colors"
              title={
                case presence_result do
                  {:ok, _} -> "Online participants"
                  _ -> "Presence unavailable"
                end
              }
            >
              <span class="hero-user-group size-4"></span>
              <%= case presence_result do %>
                <% {:ok, list} -> %>
                  <span>{length(list)}</span>
                <% _ -> %>
                  <span class="inline-block size-2 rounded-full bg-neutral"></span>
                  <%!-- Below `sm` the label is dropped: the icon plus the neutral
                        dot carry the state, and the popover keeps the full
                        sentence. A 20-character label wraps at 375px and doubles
                        the header row, and chrome must not change page geometry
                        when it breaks. Truncating is not the fix — an ellipsis on
                        a status label reads as a rendering fault. Never a number
                        in this branch, at any width. --%>
                  <span class="hidden sm:inline">Presence unavailable</span>
              <% end %>
            </button>

            <div
              :if={assigns[:show_presence_panel] && @show_presence_panel}
              class="absolute right-0 top-full mt-1 z-50 w-56 max-w-[calc(100vw-1rem)] rounded-lg border border-edge bg-base-100 shadow-lg p-2"
            >
              <div class="text-[10px] uppercase tracking-widest text-base-content/40 px-2 mb-1">
                Participants
              </div>
              <%= case presence_result do %>
                <% {:ok, []} -> %>
                  <div
                    data-test-id="shell-presence-empty"
                    class="py-1.5 px-2 text-sm text-base-content/50"
                  >
                    No one else online
                  </div>
                <% {:ok, list} -> %>
                  <div
                    :for={user <- list}
                    data-test-id="shell-presence-row"
                    class="flex items-center gap-2 py-1.5 px-2 rounded"
                  >
                    <span class="inline-block size-2 rounded-full bg-success"></span>
                    <span class="text-sm text-base-content/70">{user.name}</span>
                  </div>
                <% _ -> %>
                  <div
                    data-test-id="shell-presence-unavailable"
                    class="flex items-center gap-2 py-1.5 px-2 text-sm text-base-content/50"
                  >
                    <span class="inline-block size-2 rounded-full bg-neutral"></span>
                    <span>Presence unavailable</span>
                  </div>
              <% end %>
            </div>
          </div>
        </.async_result>

        <%!-- Dark/light mode toggle --%>
        <button
          id="theme-toggle"
          phx-hook="ThemeToggle"
          class="rounded-lg p-1 text-base-content/60 hover:bg-base-300 hover:text-base-content transition-colors"
          aria-label="Toggle dark/light mode"
          title="Toggle dark/light mode"
        >
          <span data-icon="sun" class="hero-sun size-4 hidden"></span>
          <span data-icon="moon" class="hero-moon size-4"></span>
        </button>

        <span class="text-xs text-base-content/50">{@current_user}</span>
        <.link
          href="/auth/logout"
          class="text-xs text-base-content/40 hover:text-base-content/70 transition-colors"
          title="Log out"
        >
          <span class="hero-arrow-right-on-rectangle size-4"></span>
        </.link>
      </div>
    </header>
    <%!-- Flash messages --%>
    <.flash_group flash={@flash} />
    <%!-- Surface. `flex-1` claims the remaining vertical space inside
         the inner column; `overflow-hidden` keeps the surface itself
         from scrolling (per-LiveView panels supply their own
         `overflow-y-auto` scrollers). `overscroll-contain` stops iOS
         rubber-band from propagating up and revealing/displacing the
         chrome (top app bar, bottom-tab nav).

         `min-h-0` is the load-bearing addition (post-#333): inside the
         shell's flex-col inner content column, flex items default to
         `min-height: auto` which means <main> refuses to shrink below
         its intrinsic content height. With a long chat thread that
         intrinsic height blows past the viewport — pushing the
         bottom-tab nav (the next flex sibling) off-screen on iOS Safari
         / PWA standalone even though the shell root is correctly
         100dvh-bounded. min-h-0 lets <main> actually receive the
         bounded `flex-1` height so its internal scrollers
         (#message-list etc.) can scroll on their own. --%>
    <main
      data-scroll-container
      class="flex-1 min-h-0 overflow-hidden overscroll-contain"
    >
      {@inner_content}
    </main>

    <%!-- Mobile bottom-rail (task 019e389b Stage 3) — keys off `current_path`
         + `root_user` from the same single source of truth as the desktop
         icon-rail above. lg:hidden inside the component itself so desktop
         continues to use the icon-rail. Tasks-badge wiring is parked: the
         old `<.bottom_nav>` rendered it; the icon-rail rev re-introduces
         badge support in a later stage (badge data assign `@tasks_count`
         still computed in `shell_live.ex` for the future). --%>
    <.bottom_rail current_path={@current_path} root_user={@root_user} />

    <%!-- Mobile More sheet (task 019e1322 Stage 2) — bottom sheet that surfaces
         the long-tail destinations (Skills, Changelog, Agent Resources, Org
         Context, Usage, Admin·Prompts, Admin·Federation) plus the profile +
         sign-out row. Triggered by `phx-click="open_more_sheet"` on the More
         tab in <.bottom_nav>. lg:hidden inside the component itself. --%>
    <.more_sheet
      open={@more_sheet_open}
      current_user={@current_user}
      root_user={@root_user}
    />

    <%!-- Persistent meeting bar (survives navigation) --%>
    <.live_component
      module={PlatformWeb.MeetingBarLive}
      id="meeting-bar"
      meeting_active={@meeting_active}
      meeting_space_id={@meeting_space_id}
      meeting_space_name={@meeting_space_name}
      meeting_space_slug={@meeting_space_slug}
      meeting_slug={@meeting_slug}
      meeting_started_at={@meeting_started_at}
      on_meeting_page={@on_meeting_page}
      sidebar_collapsed={@sidebar_collapsed}
    />

    <%!-- Shell-level LiveKit reconnect supervisor (task 019ee06b stage 2).
         The watchdog is a slim sibling of MeetingClient that owns ONLY
         network-survival (idempotent reconnect listener bind + breadcrumb)
         so an ICE blip while the user is on /tasks, /activity, etc. does
         not leave the surviving Room without a state observer. Gated by
         `:if={@meeting_active}` — when no meeting is active the element is
         absent from the DOM, guaranteeing zero spurious WebSocket activity.
         `phx-update="ignore"` keeps the element stable across the shell's
         frequent repatches (path changes, presence) so we don't burn
         mount() cycles relying on the singleton guard to back off. --%>
    <div
      :if={@meeting_active}
      id="meeting-watchdog"
      phx-hook="MeetingWatchdog"
      phx-update="ignore"
      class="hidden"
      data-room-state="unknown"
    >
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
